Sixth Disease - Roseola Infantum: Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
High fever resolving abruptly, Rosy-pink maculopapular truncal rash post-defervescence, Nagayama spots, Frequent febrile seizures

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-050
Reason for Visit: Sudden eruption of a full-body rash immediately following several days of high, unexplained fever in an infant, requiring urgent assessment to rule out severe bacterial infection or drug allergy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (infant) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated highly anxious parents of a 14-month-old infant who had a very high fever for 4 days that suddenly broke, immediately followed by the rapid spread of a pink rash over the torso. Urgent physical assessment is required to definitively diagnose a benign viral exanthem, rule out life-threatening meningococcal septicaemia, and provide crucial parental reassurance prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Parent states: "His fever finally broke after four terrifying days, but now his whole body has suddenly broken out in this red rash."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 4 days ago with an abrupt onset of an extremely high fever (frequently exceeding 39.5C) in an otherwise healthy-appearing 14-month-old infant. The parents report that despite the high fever, the infant was generally active and feeding adequately between fever spikes. The fever strictly lasted for 4 days. Today, there was a complete and sudden resolution of the high fever (defervescence). Precisely coinciding with the fever breaking, a distinct, discrete, rosy-pink maculopapular rash erupted initially on the trunk and has subsequently spread to the neck and extremities. The infant has had no seizures, but the parents were terrified by the fever height. No new foods or medications (other than paracetamol) have been given.
Functional Status: Alert, active, and playful, completely contrasting with the parents' high anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme parental anxiety regarding the sudden rash following the prolonged high fever.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/60 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 (Afebrile) | RR: 24 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 10 Kg
General: Well-appearing, active, playful toddler in absolutely no acute distress.
Physical Exam: Skin exam reveals a florid, discrete, rosy-pink maculopapular rash predominantly on the trunk, spreading mildly to the neck and proximal arms. Crucially, the rash characteristically blanches completely upon applied direct physical pressure (the "glass test" is reassuringly negative for petechiae). Oropharyngeal exam reveals Nagayama spots (distinct erythematous papules strictly on the soft palate and uvula). Ear exam shows mild distinct tympanic membrane erythema bilaterally without bulging or purulent acute otitis media. Neck exam reveals mildly palpable postauricular lymphadenopathy. Neurological exam is completely normal. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: High fever resolving abruptly, Rosy-pink maculopapular truncal rash post-defervescence, Nagayama spots, and Frequent febrile seizures (absent in this specific case, but a known key risk factor).
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol/ibuprofen, which can now be ceased.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sixth Disease - Roseola Infantum (HHV-6) - Status: Resolving
Differentials:

Meningococcal Septicaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rash completely blanches under pressure, the child is completely afebrile and actively playful, and entirely lacks the toxic, lethargic appearance of septicaemia.

Measles (Rubeola) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of the classic 3 Cs (cough, coryza, severe conjunctivitis), and crucially, the fever in measles worsens when the rash appears, whereas in Roseola, the fever abruptly vanishes.

Amoxicillin Rash (Drug Eruption) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No history of recent antibiotic use, and the classic timeline of fever defervescence immediately preceding the rash is pathognomonic for Roseola.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High parental anxiety resolved by clinical diagnosis)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the absolute classic, pathognomonic timeline for Roseola Infantum: a prolonged, high fever in a well-appearing infant that abruptly breaks, immediately followed by a blanching truncal rash. Despite the entirely benign nature of this viral exanthem, the sudden appearance of a full-body rash following days of high fever is a terrifying event for parents, raising immense fears of meningitis or severe allergy. An urgent, direct clinical examination was strictly required to physically perform the 'glass test' (confirming the rash blanches and is not purpuric), visually confirm the child's non-toxic state, and provide definitive, expert clinical reassurance. This intervention prevented a highly inappropriate and traumatic late-night presentation to a pediatric emergency department, perfectly fulfilling the holistic care mandate of the urgent after-hours criteria (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provide definitive clinical reassurance that the illness has already peaked and the child is now actively recovering. Advise immediate cessation of regular antipyretics (paracetamol/ibuprofen) as the fever phase has permanently resolved for this illness. No further investigations or medications are required.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ceasing the regular temperature checks and antipyretic administration tonight, the parents will allow themselves and the child a full, uninterrupted night of sleep; waking up to a happy, active child will powerfully reinforce the clinical reassurance that the emergency has passed.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only required if the child suddenly develops a new, high fever or becomes acutely lethargic.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the child develops a new, high fever, if the rash changes to dark purple spots that do not fade when pressed with a clear glass, or if the child becomes unusually floppy, extremely sleepy, or difficult to wake.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is entirely clinical based on the classic sequence of symptoms; no blood tests are needed.
Clinical Course: The appearance of the rash actually signals the absolute end of the infection. The rash is entirely harmless, does not itch, and will fade away completely on its own over the next 1 to 3 days without peeling or scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The danger period has passed. The rash is just his immune system clearing out the last of the virus. However, if he ever develops a rash that looks like dark purple bruises that do not disappear when you press on them, you must seek emergency care immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is a classic childhood virus called Roseola. The hallmark of this virus is that the fever stays very high for 3-4 days, but the child acts relatively normal. The exact moment the immune system beats the virus, the fever breaks, and it leaves behind this pink rash. He is no longer contagious, and he does not need any more medication.
Actionable Steps: Stop the paracetamol and ibuprofen. Let him eat, drink, and sleep normally. You can send him back to daycare once the rash has completely faded.

SMS INFORMATION

Confirmed Roseola (harmless childhood virus). The fever breaking and the rash appearing means the illness is over. Stop all fever medications. No further treatment needed.

SMS EDUCATION

The rash will fade on its own in 1-3 days. Go to the ED only if he develops a brand new high fever or a dark purple rash that does not fade when pressed.

